1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,176
Three B links collaborative practice, Crew Resource Management,
quality assurance and just culture. They are not separate slogans:
each describes how a team prevents, detects or responds to failure.

2
00:00:13,176 --> 00:00:26,208
We will follow an original crew from the shift briefing through a
changing trauma call and a simulated medication near miss. The cases
rehearse the school objectives without using its assessed scenario
bodies.

3
00:00:26,208 --> 00:00:36,240
The important performance is observable: a role is assigned, a concern
is heard, an unsafe action stops and a report leads to a practical
correction.

4
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:49,488
Learning from an event does not remove accountability, and
accountability does not require blaming a person for every bad
outcome. Keep behavior, context and outcome distinct throughout this
lesson.

5
00:00:49,488 --> 00:01:01,416
A crew may appear busy while important needs remain uncovered. One
person sets up equipment, another asks a history and neither notices
that no one owns the immediate airway problem.

6
00:01:01,416 --> 00:01:12,624
The school contrasts simple side-by-side cooperation with true
collaboration: coordinated work, clear communication and shared
decision-making toward the patient's needs.

7
00:01:12,624 --> 00:01:22,560
A shared goal does not mean everyone performs the same task. It means
the separate tasks fit together and the team can explain their
priorities and dependencies.

8
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:33,504
Ask a partner to state the current objective in one sentence. If your
answers differ materially, reconcile them before the mismatch produces
a missed or duplicated action.

9
00:01:33,504 --> 00:01:42,456
Name the person and the task instead of announcing that someone should
do it. Include the immediate priority and the relevant safety
constraint.

10
00:01:42,456 --> 00:01:51,504
The receiver acknowledges and clarifies uncertainty. An assigned task
without acknowledgement may exist only in the leader's mind.

11
00:01:51,504 --> 00:02:00,024
The leader monitors whether the workload is feasible and whether the
patient has changed. Delegating a task does not mean the leader can
forget it.

12
00:02:00,024 --> 00:02:12,048
Completion, inability to perform and a new hazard all return to the
team. Reassign explicitly when roles change so that ongoing care does
not silently lose its owner.

13
00:02:12,048 --> 00:02:22,440
A shared mental model is a common understanding of the patient, risks
and plan. It is not a demand that all team members hold the same
unchanging diagnosis.

14
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:35,016
In the original trauma call, one clinician notices worsening pallor
and a weaker pulse while the other is completing a secondary survey.
Without an update, they are treating two different mental patients.

15
00:02:35,016 --> 00:02:46,584
Announce the new finding and its significance, then state the revised
priority. Confirm who continues support, who requests resources and
how the movement plan changes.

16
00:02:46,584 --> 00:02:56,112
Invite a challenge to the new explanation. A shared model is useful
because it is visible and revisable, not because it prevents
disagreement.

17
00:02:56,112 --> 00:03:07,248
Situational awareness includes the patient, the environment and the
crew. A monitor may remain stable while an exit becomes blocked or a
partner becomes overwhelmed.

18
00:03:07,248 --> 00:03:17,640
Watch for fixation on a procedure or diagnosis. If both clinicians are
occupied with the same detail, ask who is watching the broader patient
and scene.

19
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:30,024
Share uncertainty early and anticipate the next likely need. A short
request for help before overload is safer than silently falling behind
until a critical task is missed.

20
00:03:30,024 --> 00:03:41,952
Use protocols and team input to make timely decisions, then reassess
the result. Awareness without an action or plan update is only
noticing, not effective CRM.

21
00:03:41,952 --> 00:03:53,208
A leader gives direction while admitting limits and inviting
information. Saying please tell me if I miss something is useful only
when the response to a real concern is respectful.

22
00:03:53,208 --> 00:04:04,632
Followership is not silent obedience. It includes completing tasks,
reporting changes, supporting the plan and challenging a safety
problem regardless of experience or rank.

23
00:04:04,632 --> 00:04:17,232
Flattening hierarchy reduces the barrier to speaking up; it does not
abolish clinical responsibility or expand anyone's scope. Keep the
responsible roles and required supervision clear.

24
00:04:17,232 --> 00:04:29,136
When a junior member is right about a hazard, acknowledge the
contribution and change the plan. Defending status at the expense of
patient safety teaches the crew to hide the next concern.

25
00:04:29,136 --> 00:04:41,304
The module includes police, fire, hospital staff and appropriate
bystanders in collaborative practice. Each may hold information or
expertise that the ambulance crew does not.

26
00:04:41,304 --> 00:04:52,344
Explain the patient's need and listen to the other discipline's
constraints. An urgent care request and a rescue hazard must be
reconciled into a workable sequence.

27
00:04:52,344 --> 00:05:03,360
Respect does not mean avoiding a difficult conversation. Describe a
specific conflict and seek a patient-centered solution rather than
accusing another agency of not caring.

28
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:13,680
Confirm who owns the next step and communicate any delay or changed
risk. Several agencies working hard do not guarantee that the critical
task has an owner.

29
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:23,592
A brief safety huddle makes important information visible before
pressure increases. Discuss readiness, known limitations and who can
provide help.

30
00:05:23,592 --> 00:05:34,176
Identify missing or unreliable equipment through the actual service
process. Do not let an informal workaround silently become the crew's
accepted standard.

31
00:05:34,176 --> 00:05:44,736
Agree on roles and likely hazards, while leaving room for new
information. A briefing is a starting model, not a script that must
survive every change.

32
00:05:44,736 --> 00:05:54,624
Ask directly for uncertainty and thank the person who raises it.
Repeated respectful responses make psychological safety credible in
daily work.

33
00:05:54,624 --> 00:06:08,400
The tools overlap, but they are not interchangeable acronyms. Choose
one based on whether the problem is a task, critical information, an
urgent change, a structured update or an unheard safety concern.

34
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:19,056
A long SBAR is not the first response to a medicine about to be
administered incorrectly. A direct stop can protect the patient,
followed by a structured explanation.

35
00:06:19,056 --> 00:06:29,040
Likewise, a shouted callout does not replace the detailed handover
needed at transfer. The receiver needs enough context to continue care
safely.

36
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:38,424
Practice the actual words with a partner. Knowing an acronym's
expansion is not evidence that you can use the tool under interruption
or noise.

37
00:06:38,424 --> 00:06:48,240
Use a named receiver and a specific request. For an original example:
Alex, obtain a repeat manual blood pressure and report it now.

38
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:57,528
The receiver repeats the essential task, and the sender confirms or
corrects it. This catches a misheard action before it becomes a wrong
action.

39
00:06:57,528 --> 00:07:07,968
When completed, report the actual result and any problem. If the
measurement cannot be obtained, report that instead of silently
attempting forever.

40
00:07:07,968 --> 00:07:16,728
Read-back confirms the message, not the clinical appropriateness of
the instruction. A correctly repeated unsafe plan must still be
questioned.

41
00:07:16,728 --> 00:07:27,960
In a simulated medication task, two people repeat the same volume but
neither reads the concentration. The communication is consistent, yet
the clinical check has failed.

42
00:07:27,960 --> 00:07:37,896
An independent check uses the original patient, order or protocol and
actual product information. It does not start by accepting the first
person's answer.

43
00:07:37,896 --> 00:07:49,056
Read back critical numbers with units and clarify ambiguity. Dose,
concentration and volume answer different questions even when one
number happens to look familiar.

44
00:07:49,056 --> 00:07:57,648
Use both tools where required: confirm the message and verify the
plan. Team agreement is not a substitute for evidence.

45
00:07:57,648 --> 00:08:07,200
A callout is a brief direct statement of an urgent need or change. It
helps the team notice information that might otherwise remain with one
person.

46
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:19,632
In the trauma case, say that the patient is less responsive and the
pulse is weaker, then request immediate reassessment and the relevant
support. Avoid a vague announcement that things look bad.

47
00:08:19,632 --> 00:08:30,072
Acknowledge the callout and state the change in plan. If no one
responds, the sender should verify it was heard rather than assuming
silence means agreement.

48
00:08:30,072 --> 00:08:40,392
Keep the tone calm and the content concise. Urgency is carried by
clear findings and action, not by humiliation or competing shouts.

49
00:08:40,392 --> 00:08:48,984
Lead with the current problem and urgency. A listener should not need
to wait through a long history to discover that the patient is
deteriorating.

50
00:08:48,984 --> 00:08:57,528
Give the background that changes the decision, then your assessment
and evidence. Separate what you measured from what you think it means.

51
00:08:57,528 --> 00:09:07,368
Include treatment and response, not only the first set of
observations. Make a specific recommendation or request within the
actual clinical pathway.

52
00:09:07,368 --> 00:09:19,320
Confirm the response and clarify uncertain instructions. SBAR
organizes communication; it does not grant a new treatment authority
or guarantee that a message was received.

53
00:09:19,320 --> 00:09:29,352
The school PACE model expands to Probe, Alert, Challenge and
Emergency. It is a graded-assertiveness tool for an unresolved safety
concern.

54
00:09:29,352 --> 00:09:39,192
Use progressively explicit language if the concern is not addressed.
Name the risk and the action needed instead of repeating an
increasingly frustrated hint.

55
00:09:39,192 --> 00:09:48,984
The goal is safe action, not winning a disagreement. Once the unsafe
step is paused, verify the facts together and revise the plan.

56
00:09:48,984 --> 00:09:58,200
The stages are not a mandatory slow sequence. If a harmful
administration is imminent, use the direct emergency stop immediately.

57
00:09:58,200 --> 00:10:07,920
In the original near-miss case, the junior partner notices a
concentration mismatch while the senior clinician is preparing to
deliver a simulated medicine.

58
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:19,656
The safe response is to pause and hear the concern, not to defend
experience. A statement that anyone may speak up is meaningless if the
first actual challenge is punished.

59
00:10:19,656 --> 00:10:29,424
Check the source information, product, intended amount and patient
before resuming. Do not merely change the volume to the second
person's guess.

60
00:10:29,424 --> 00:10:40,128
Acknowledge the intercept and report the near miss through the
appropriate process. The team protected this patient, but the
confusing system may remain for the next crew.

61
00:10:40,128 --> 00:10:50,640
Conflict language should describe a specific behavior or unresolved
fact. General attacks invite a dispute about personality instead of
correcting the action.

62
00:10:50,640 --> 00:11:00,624
Use an observation, a concern and a clear request. The request should
say what needs to happen now, not merely express dissatisfaction.

63
00:11:00,624 --> 00:11:10,560
Respectful language can still be firm. Immediate safety does not
require an apologetic hint, especially after a clear concern has been
ignored.

64
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:21,456
Resolve the urgent hazard first and address broader disagreement
afterward through the appropriate channel. Do not turn an active
clinical problem into a prolonged argument.

65
00:11:21,456 --> 00:11:32,592
Give feedback soon enough to be useful and focus on an observed
action. Explain its consequence and a practical next step rather than
judging the person's character.

66
00:11:32,592 --> 00:11:42,168
When receiving feedback, listen and ask what specifically was
observed. You can clarify context without treating every concern as a
personal attack.

67
00:11:42,168 --> 00:11:56,592
Acknowledge useful information and identify a change you can
demonstrate. If a proposed correction conflicts with the actual
protocol or device, resolve that evidence rather than agreeing
politely and remaining confused.

68
00:11:56,592 --> 00:12:06,552
Repeat the relevant sequence and check whether performance changes. A
friendly debrief is valuable, but the safety target is a more reliable
action.

69
00:12:06,552 --> 00:12:18,216
The school uses the Swiss Cheese Model to explain interacting
weaknesses across defensive layers. A final wrong action may be
visible while the contributing conditions are less obvious.

70
00:12:18,216 --> 00:12:31,992
In the original near miss, similar labels, crowded storage,
interrupted checking and an authority gradient all matter. Correcting
only the person at the syringe may leave the same trap in place.

71
00:12:31,992 --> 00:12:44,328
System analysis does not mean every event has identical causes or no
individual responsibility. Investigate the actual event rather than
forcing every detail into a diagram.

72
00:12:44,328 --> 00:12:57,024
Ask which defenses existed, why they did not work and what could
reliably interrupt recurrence. More reminders alone may be weaker than
correcting confusing storage or unavailable equipment.

73
00:12:57,024 --> 00:13:08,208
A layered approach reduces reliance on one perfect person or one
perfect check. Design, preparation, communication and learning each
contribute something different.

74
00:13:08,208 --> 00:13:20,616
The layers are not automatically independent. If two clinicians rely
on the same wrong assumption or unreadable label, two signatures may
still represent one shared failure.

75
00:13:20,616 --> 00:13:31,104
Choose a correction that addresses the observed cause, and test
whether it creates another problem. Moving a product may reduce one
confusion but make urgent access harder.

76
00:13:31,104 --> 00:13:40,968
Use follow-up information to refine the change. The purpose of the
model is better care, not a visually satisfying explanation that no
one evaluates.

77
00:13:40,968 --> 00:13:53,832
Quality assurance is structured review of whether care meets expected
standards, using records, audits, peer review and incident
information. It is more than finding a person to criticize.

78
00:13:53,832 --> 00:14:07,200
Your role includes complete patient records, timely safety reports,
honest debriefs and receptiveness to feedback. A missing record can
hide both an unsafe process and a successful defense.

79
00:14:07,200 --> 00:14:18,360
Ask how the findings lead to action and who owns follow-up. Repeatedly
discussing the same near miss without changing the process is not a
completed improvement cycle.

80
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:30,720
Interpret report counts carefully. More reports may reflect better
willingness to speak up, not necessarily more underlying harm; compare
the reporting process and relevant care measures.

81
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:41,736
The school repeatedly describes a near miss as intercepted before
reaching the patient, but its glossary also says before harm. Those
are not identical thresholds.

82
00:14:41,736 --> 00:14:53,784
The WHO terminology separates a near miss, which did not reach the
patient, from a no-harm incident, which reached the patient without
discernible harm. A harmful incident causes harm.

83
00:14:53,784 --> 00:15:06,912
An error can be caught before administration or can reach the patient.
Do not define all errors as necessarily reaching the patient, and do
not infer no harm merely because the first observation is reassuring.

84
00:15:06,912 --> 00:15:17,376
Use the actual reporting system's fields while describing the event
precisely. Do not relabel a wrong administration as a near miss just
because the patient survived.

85
00:15:17,376 --> 00:15:30,264
Human error is an unintentional slip, lapse or mistake. A competent
person may misread a confusing label or omit a step during
interruption despite intending to follow the correct process.

86
00:15:30,264 --> 00:15:39,744
Understand what information and conditions existed at the time.
Hindsight can make an ambiguous situation seem much clearer than it
was to the provider.

87
00:15:39,744 --> 00:15:50,760
The school response is to support or console the provider, learn and
correct system contributions. Support is compatible with an honest
report and appropriate review.

88
00:15:50,760 --> 00:16:01,416
Do not classify intent from the severity of the outcome alone. The
same unintentional action may have no harm in one case and severe
consequences in another.

89
00:16:01,416 --> 00:16:13,968
At-risk behavior is a choice that increases risk when the risk is not
recognized or seems justified. A repeated shortcut may feel normal
because it has not yet caused a visible injury.

90
00:16:13,968 --> 00:16:24,264
The module gives prematurely abbreviated assessment as an example.
Investigate why the shortcut made sense to the person and what
workload or rewards may encourage it.

91
00:16:24,264 --> 00:16:36,408
Use coaching and clear expectations, while correcting the conditions
that reward the unsafe choice. Education alone may fail if the
workflow makes the safe method impractical.

92
00:16:36,408 --> 00:16:46,320
Do not automatically call every intentional deviation reckless.
Context, risk awareness and the actual choice require fair review.

93
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:58,704
The school defines reckless behavior as conscious disregard of a
substantial unjustifiable risk. This is different from an
unintentional mistake or a shortcut whose risk was not appreciated.

94
00:16:58,704 --> 00:17:09,552
If an immediate safety threat is present, protect patients and
colleagues through the appropriate process. Do not wait for a later
learning discussion before stopping unsafe practice.

95
00:17:09,552 --> 00:17:20,904
Formal review and corrective or disciplinary action may be required.
The course is not an authority for a partner to deliver an instant
legal verdict or an employment sanction.

96
00:17:20,904 --> 00:17:31,608
Evidence and context matter regardless of seniority. A good outcome
does not make a reckless choice safe, just as a bad outcome does not
prove recklessness.

97
00:17:31,608 --> 00:17:43,104
Just culture seeks learning and appropriate accountability. It
distinguishes behavior and system contributions instead of responding
only to the outcome or the person's reputation.

98
00:17:43,104 --> 00:17:56,352
Psychological safety means people can raise concerns, ask questions
and admit uncertainty without humiliation or automatic blame. It does
not remove professional responsibilities.

99
00:17:56,352 --> 00:18:09,624
Do not promise that every report has no consequences or that all
discussion is legally privileged. Follow the actual reporting and
review policy, with fair treatment and appropriate support.

100
00:18:09,624 --> 00:18:21,072
The strongest practical signal is what happens after a real concern: a
respectful pause, accurate review, visible correction and feedback to
the people who reported.

101
00:18:21,072 --> 00:18:30,960
A simulated syringe has been prepared using the wrong assumed
concentration. The partner identifies the mismatch before any
simulated medication is delivered.

102
00:18:30,960 --> 00:18:42,240
Pause, verify the patient and source information, and restore a
correct authorized preparation. Do not continue because the error was
only a small calculation issue.

103
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:53,160
This is a near miss because the incident did not reach the patient.
The intercept is a successful defense, but the confusing concentration
or storage still needs attention.

104
00:18:53,160 --> 00:19:03,240
Report through the appropriate safety process and include what enabled
the intercept. Learning from what worked is as important as describing
the initial failure.

105
00:19:03,240 --> 00:19:14,688
Change one variable: the mismatch is noticed after some simulated
medicine has already entered. The event has reached the patient even
if the person initially looks unchanged.

106
00:19:14,688 --> 00:19:30,576
Stop the implicated delivery as appropriate, assess and support the
patient, and determine the actual product, concentration, amount and
time. Uncertain delivery is stated as uncertain, not rounded into a
reassuring number.

107
00:19:30,576 --> 00:19:42,360
Obtain the appropriate urgent clinical guidance and monitoring.
Reversal is substance-specific and may not exist; do not improvise an
antidote or wait for harm before seeking help.

108
00:19:42,360 --> 00:19:54,480
If no discernible harm is established, it is a no-harm incident under
the stated terminology, not a near miss. Harm and later evolution
still require appropriate evaluation.

109
00:19:54,480 --> 00:20:05,208
The first priority after recognizing an error is patient safety.
Address the implicated action and immediate clinical need before
treating the event as paperwork.

110
00:20:05,208 --> 00:20:19,728
Notify the appropriate clinical and supervisory resources promptly,
with enough detail for useful advice. Consultation should be
concurrent with necessary immediate support, not a reason to leave the
patient unattended.

111
00:20:19,728 --> 00:20:32,040
Communicate honestly with the patient or appropriate authorized person
through the actual disclosure process. Do not delay urgent care while
trying to settle blame or a complete causal explanation.

112
00:20:32,040 --> 00:20:44,712
Then complete patient documentation and the separate safety report,
participate in review and use available support. Protecting future
patients requires a follow-through beyond the immediate recovery.

113
00:20:44,712 --> 00:20:55,272
Use plain compassionate language to explain what is known and the
immediate care plan. Acknowledge uncertainty and answer questions
honestly within your role.

114
00:20:55,272 --> 00:21:10,488
The school includes disclosure as part of responding to errors and
near misses. The actual scope and process, including near-miss
disclosure, must follow applicable organizational guidance; do not
invent one universal script.

115
00:21:10,488 --> 00:21:21,840
Family involvement depends on the patient's wishes, capacity and the
applicable authority. A relative being present is not automatic
permission to share every clinical detail.

116
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:31,032
Do not speculate about another person's motives or promise that there
will be no harm. Explain the assessment and follow-up while the facts
are established.

117
00:21:31,032 --> 00:21:43,776
Record the actual administration, observations, actions, response and
relevant consultations. Saying only medication given hides the
information the next clinician needs.

118
00:21:43,776 --> 00:21:55,296
Objective language means accurate facts and attributed information,
not removing the fact that an incorrect amount was delivered. Reasoned
clinical concerns are labelled, not erased.

119
00:21:55,296 --> 00:22:09,888
Complete the separate required safety report and notification. Under
current A zero four, PSLS reporting covers clinical deviations, safety
events, near misses and errors; it does not replace the PCR.

120
00:22:09,888 --> 00:22:23,304
Protect privacy through the approved secure channel and actual legal
framework. Correct records using the authorized process; do not
delete, backdate or rewrite the event to make it appear safer.

121
00:22:23,304 --> 00:22:35,736
The module names fear of discipline, peer judgment, lack of time and
the belief that nothing will change. These are real process barriers,
not simply a lack of professional motivation.

122
00:22:35,736 --> 00:22:47,424
Leaders can make reporting safer by explaining the process, responding
fairly and avoiding ridicule. They must not promise immunity that the
actual policy cannot guarantee.

123
00:22:47,424 --> 00:22:59,520
Make the route clear and accessible, and ensure immediate safety
information reaches the right person without waiting for a long form.
Complete the required documentation promptly afterward.

124
00:22:59,520 --> 00:23:10,800
Close the feedback loop with a visible correction and its result.
Acknowledging the report without changing a persistent hazard teaches
people that reporting has little value.

125
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:20,592
Build the timeline from actual records and participant accounts.
Separate established facts, differing recollections and remaining
uncertainty.

126
00:23:20,592 --> 00:23:27,984
Ask what the provider saw and expected at the time. Avoid assuming
that the final outcome was obvious from the beginning.

127
00:23:27,984 --> 00:23:39,192
Identify both failed and successful defenses and consider behavior
fairly. A near-miss intercept may reveal useful teamwork as well as a
system weakness.

128
00:23:39,192 --> 00:23:50,712
Finish with a practical action, an owner and a way to check change.
Offer appropriate support to involved staff without making the debrief
a forced personal disclosure exercise.

129
00:23:50,712 --> 00:24:04,488
For the original near miss, propose an approved storage and labelling
correction plus a reliable independent check. The correction should
address the observed confusion, not merely add a warning to be more
careful.

130
00:24:04,488 --> 00:24:16,176
Test it safely with training materials under realistic interruption.
Observe whether people identify the right product and detect the
mismatch rather than asking whether they like the new system.

131
00:24:16,176 --> 00:24:26,256
Look for unintended consequences such as harder access or a new look-
alike arrangement. A locally improved step can still worsen the
overall workflow.

132
00:24:26,256 --> 00:24:37,152
Record the result and revise the change as needed. Reporting,
respectful review and measured correction together create the safer
next call described by the module.

133
00:24:37,152 --> 00:24:47,280
Be able to assign roles and update the team when the patient or scene
changes. Invite concerns and show that a junior voice can stop an
unsafe action.

134
00:24:47,280 --> 00:24:58,944
Demonstrate closed loop, a useful callout, a structured SBAR and
appropriate PACE escalation. Do not force a slow sequence when an
immediate stop is needed.

135
00:24:58,944 --> 00:25:10,560
Distinguish human error, at-risk behavior and recklessness using
evidence and context. Separately identify whether the incident reached
the patient and whether harm occurred.

136
00:25:10,560 --> 00:25:23,568
Finally, protect the patient, communicate honestly, complete the
correct records and report, then help test a practical improvement.
That is the complete safety cycle, not merely a promise to try harder.
